Using `edits` is not a reason to avoid touching an existing file. It is what makes touching one
safe, and wiring new code into the application almost always means editing an existing file:
registering a route where the other routes are registered, rendering a component where the other
components are rendered, exporting a helper from the module that already exports its neighbours.
A change that adds only new files is usually unfinished, because nothing in the running
application refers to what you added. Make that connecting edit with a small `edits` entry.

Prefer `edits` for every existing file. Whole-file `content` for a file that already exists means
re-typing every line you are not changing, and lines that are re-typed are lines that go missing:
a homepage tile was once added by returning a file that kept the new tile and silently dropped the
two hundred and seventy lines of tiles already on the page. Send `content` for an existing file
only when you genuinely intend to replace all of it, and say so in your `summary`.

