When behavior depends on time passing, such as a poll interval, a refresh, or a timeout, test it
with the fake-timer facility the repository's test framework already provides and advance the
clock explicitly. Never let a test wait for real time to elapse: the suite's own timeout ends the
run first and the required validation fails.
A test must not open a real database, cache, queue or network connection, and must not leave a
timer, listener or handle open when it finishes. Importing an application entry point often opens
all of them as a side effect of being imported, and the suite then hangs until the runner kills it
-- which is reported as a timeout, not as a connection, so it is easy to misread as a slow test
and answer by writing the same test again. If exercising a route or a page end to end would boot
the whole application, mount only the piece under test, or substitute the module that connects,
using whatever the repository's existing tests already do for this. A handler exercised through a
request and response double is worth more than an end-to-end test that cannot finish.

Mounting the application's own real entry point can satisfy that same boundary and still hang for
a reason that has nothing to do with the connection this test is actually about: the entry point's
own wiring may construct other clients -- a mail service, a queue, a payment gateway -- that this
change never touches, and the suite hangs on whichever of those has no working credential in this
environment. Prefer the narrowest real boundary that exercises what changed -- the specific route,
handler, or page under test -- over the whole assembled application, even when a real boundary,
not a wholesale mock, is exactly what the change calls for.

Substituting that connection is not only about finishing on time: when the code you changed calls
through a shared, already-configured client -- an HTTP client with its own base URL, a database or
queue connection the repository wires up once and reuses -- a test that replaces the whole calling
module can prove your code called something, but not that what actually left the client was
correct. A base URL and a request path were once each right on their own and wrong together,
joining into a route that did not exist, and only a test substituting the connection at its own
boundary -- the transport a real client would use, not the module that calls it -- would have
caught it. Prefer that boundary when this repository's tests already establish one; add it there
in the same shape as the repository's existing tests for a comparable call, rather than mocking
the calling module wholesale.

The same reasoning applies when the connection you are replacing is your own change's
persistence or execution call, not only an outbound client: a test that replaces the exact
model, repository, or executor call a new code path introduces -- so that only the mock's own
call arguments are asserted -- proves the call was made, never that the record it was supposed
to create, reload, or forward downstream actually exists. When a requirement is to persist,
reload, or forward something, prefer a test that reads back what the real configured
persistence layer now holds, in the same shape this repository's own existing tests for a
comparable effect already use, rather than asserting only that a mocked method was called with
the right arguments.
When your change has to call a module this repository already provides, read that module in the
snapshot and call what it actually exports, by name. Never probe for an export shape at runtime,
never wrap the import in a fallback that tries several names in turn, and never answer with an
error status because a module was not the shape you expected. A guard like that replaces the
behavior the requirement asked for with a runtime failure that the repository's own tests then
observe. If the snapshot does not show you a module the task depends on, implement everything
that does not depend on it and say plainly in your summary which module you could not see,
rather than guessing at its interface.
The same care extends to every identifier your new code touches, not only a module's export
shape: a symbol used without a matching import in the file that uses it -- a well-known
library, a helper defined elsewhere, a name a test's own setup happens to provide globally --
is undefined in exactly the file the lint gate checks. Re-read each file you return once
against exactly this before calling it finished.
The same is true when a requirement asks you to reuse an existing cross-cutting mechanism --
authentication, request-forgery protection, rate-limiting, logging -- rather than a single
module: search the snapshot for it by what it does before writing a new one. A second
mechanism placed beside the first is never equivalent to it, even when it is meant to behave
the same way, because nothing then proves the two agree, and applying a newly-written one to
an existing route changes behavior the requirement never asked you to touch. If the snapshot
does not show you where this repository's version of it lives, say so plainly in your summary
rather than substituting your own.

An error your change catches must never reach a client, a log a client's own request can
trigger, or a record that is itself returned or listed back to a client, in its raw form -- a
caught exception's own message, a stack frame, or a downstream query string can carry
infrastructure detail no client-facing surface is supposed to expose. Route it through this
repository's existing centralized, sanitized error handling the same way every other failure in
this area already does, rather than writing a response, a stored field, or a log line that
bypasses it.

When a change introduces a persistent or queued unit of work -- a record waiting to be
processed, a job another step will pick up -- that work must still be reachable after the
process handling it restarts or exits, or by a different worker than the one that created it.
An in-memory promise chain, callback, or variable is not durable: if that is the only thing
resuming the work, a restart or a crash strands it, often permanently, with nothing left
recording that it needs to be retried. Prefer whatever durable claiming or retry mechanism this
repository's own existing queued or background work already uses.

When a change introduces a test credential, token, key, or secret-shaped literal, give it an
obviously placeholder shape -- a `test-`/`fake-`/`mock-` prefix, for example -- rather than
inventing a fresh arbitrary string. This repository's own tooling treats any sufficiently
random-looking literal as a possible real secret and withholds it from review on sight --
unconditionally, with no retry able to recover it -- exactly as it must for an actual credential.
A fixture that reads as a placeholder to a person should read as one to that scan too.

When a requirement depends on a real backing service -- a database, cache, or queue -- that this
environment does not actually provide, mock it the way this repository's own tests already mock
an external API or database call elsewhere -- openly, not as a disguised stand-in -- and say so
explicitly, in the test itself and in your summary, rather than trying a real connection first
and silently falling back to an in-memory substitute when none answers. Either shape is a mock;
the difference that matters is whether it is labelled as one. A test that presents a mocked round
trip as though it were real still claims to prove the real effect, and rewriting it in different
words each attempt does not change that.
